  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Aside from potential for nocturnal fog \ndevelopment tonight and Tuesday night, generally tranquil \nweather is expected to prevail through the middle of the week.\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place within the vicinity of our \narea through the middle of the week. Dry weather is expected to \ncontinue through Wednesday amid warming temperatures each day.\n\nScattered to broken high cloud cover will persist through the \nmiddle of the week despite surface high pressure. Temperatures \nare expected to increase back to above normal values, warming \neach day through Wednesday. High temperatures look to be in the \nupper 60s to low 70s Tuesday, and in the low to mid 70s \nWednesday. Overnights will becoming increasingly milder (no \nfrost/freeze potential). Dry weather is expected to persist \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nThere is potential for areas of fog development both tonight \nand Tuesday night. Dewpoints will also be on the rise each day. \nGiven mostly clear skies, light winds, and long nights, we \nanticipate some fog development across the area during the \novernight periods. The latest hi-res guidance suggests the lower\nvisibilities (and thus higher dense fog probabilities) will be \nalong and southeast of the I-95 corridor and across the coastal \nplain. Confidence remains low on any dense fog, but trends will \ncontinue to be monitored. Where fog does develop, it will likely\nbe slow to mix out after sunrise Tuesday and Wednesday \nmornings. Light southerly flow will promote dewpoint advection \nand the solar insolation is relatively weak until closer to \nmidday, which will limit mixing earlier in the morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next chance for rain returns late this week and \ninto the weekend.\n\nThe ridging that has be in place across the area for the past \nseveral days will finally begin to break down by Wednesday night \ninto Thursday as a potent upper trough over central CONUS cuts off \nand lifts northeastward into portions of the Great Lakes and \nsouthern Canada by the end of the week. As this occurs, broad \ntroughing is expected to take shape across the eastern CONUS as a \nfew lobes of upper level energy will pivot through the region \nThursday and over the weekend. At the surface, low pressure over the \nMidwest is forecast to track northeast into Canada as it weakens \ninto Thursday with a trailing cold front back to the southwest. \nAfter this time, there is still considerable uncertainty as to how \nthis system will evolve to impact our local area.\n\nAt the very least, broad low pressure looks to lift well to the \nnorth and west of the area Thursday into Friday. This is expected to \nbring rain chances back to the region, as a cold front slowly \napproaches the area. Latest guidance indicates that the frontal \nprogression has slowed, meaning precipitation now looks to occur \nThursday afternoon into Friday. PoPs are generally in the 50-70% \nrange for most of the area with greatest PoPs further north and \nwest. However, the better upper level support as depicted by the NAM \n(in its last days in operation) and other global guidance remains \ndisplaced to the N+W of the area, it should be noted that these PoPs \nmay in fact be a bit too high.  \n\nHeading into this weekend, ensemble guidance indicates negative \nanomalies and lowered surface pressure across the region. This \nindicates that the pattern may remain a bit unsettled through the \nweekend and into early next week. This is supported by NBM PoPs \nwhich as a 20-30% chance for showers on Saturday, less than 10% of \nprecipitation on Sunday, and 30-40% PoPs returning on Monday.",
